    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 111859\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1159 AM PDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Breezy south-southwesterly winds expected across the region, \n   potentially impacting boaters and resulting in increased fire \n   danger.\n\n*  Chances for precipitation do not return until at least early next \n   week but remain low and uncertain. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Wednesday.\n\n\nAn upper level trough will dig into the region from the Northwest US \nthrough the weekend. The center of the system will remain to our \nnorth, so it will remain dry with no rain chances through the \nweekend. As the pressure gradient increases with the passing system, \nsouth to southwest winds will also increase. Widespread gusts 25-35 \nMPH are expected each afternoon with the strongest winds expected on \nSaturday. Isolated gusts to 40 MPH are possible, especially in the \nWestern Mojave Desert where NBM shows spotty 50%+ probabilities for \ngusts over 40 MPH, but widespread wind impacts are not expected. As \nsoutherly flow sets up in the Colorado River Valley, impactful winds \nand waves are possible this weekend on Lake Mead and Lake Mohave. \nWith a dry airmass and breezy winds, fires could easily start and \nspread. As the trough lifts out early next week, winds will \ngradually decrease each afternoon. Around midweek, gusts will drop \nto 20 MPH or less.  \n\nOnce the trough exits early next week, high pressure will try to \nreestablish itself. Heights will build back west, but with strong \nzonal flow aloft still, it will struggle. Some moisture may sneak \nnorth into western Arizona and the forecast has low chances for \nthunderstorms in portions of Mohave County starting Tuesday \nafternoon. With uncertainty in how the area of high pressure sets \nup, low confidence in thunderstorm chances next week. Temperatures \nthis weekend will drop to near normal, and with only weak high rises \nnext week temperatures should remain near normal through the period. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast \nPackage...Breezy southerly to southwesterly winds will continue \nthrough the TAF period along with wind gusts in the 15 to 25 knot \nrange. VFR conditions will persist through the TAF period. \nTemperatures will exceed through 02Z. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty southerly to \nsouthwesterly winds will pick up across the region early this \nafternoon with peak wind gusts in the 20 to 25 knot range for most\nlocations. Stronger, westerly winds are expected across the \nwestern Mojave Desert, including KDAG, with peak gusts in the 25 \nto 30 knot range. Northwesterly winds in the northern Owens \nValley, will briefly increase out of the west later this afternoon\nbefore shifting back to the northwest after sunset. FEW to SCT \nmid and high clouds will filter through the area. Isolated \nthunderstorms may develop this afternoon over parts of Mohave and \nLincoln counties, but these should not impact any terminals. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...The Fire Weather Watch has been upgraded to a Red \nFlag Warning and is in effect from 11AM Saturday through midnight \nfor Nye, Esmeralda, and portions of Lincoln and Clark counties. \nConcerns include RH ranging from 5 to 15% while winds increase to 25 \nMPH and gusts to around 35 MPH. Winds will continue into the early \novernight as humidities begin to recover by late that night.\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
